items/def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative.md:120:## Proof
items/def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative.md:122:1.1 On a principal chart, each $p\in\pi^{-1}(U_i)$ has a unique expression $p=s_i(x)h$. The associated-bundle chart therefore assigns $[s_i(x)h,v]$ the vector coordinate $\rho(h)v$. In particular $[s_i(x),\psi_i(x)]$ determines a unique smooth component $\psi_i$. If $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, then $[s_j,\psi_j]=[s_i,\rho(g_{ij})\psi_j]$; equality with $[s_i,\psi_i]$ gives $\psi_j=\rho(g_{ij}^{-1})\psi_i$. [F1, F2, F3, given]
items/def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative.md:124:1.2 In a finite basis of $V$, $\operatorname{GL}_{\mathbb C}(V)$ is the open subset where the determinant is nonzero, so its tangent space at the identity is $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(V)$. Thus the differential $\rho_*$ is real-linear by [F6]. For fixed $g$, [F9] gives $\rho\circ C_{g^{-1}}=C_{\rho(g^{-1})}\circ\rho$. The derivative of target conjugation is the linear map $T\mapsto\rho(g^{-1})T\rho(g)$, while the derivative of source conjugation is $\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}$ by [F7]. The chain rule [F6] therefore gives $\rho_*(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Z)=\rho(g^{-1})\rho_*(Z)\rho(g)$. For a smooth transition $g_{ij}:U_i\cap U_j\to G$, differentiation of $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$ decomposes a tangent vector into the right translate of the tangent from $s_i$ and the fundamental vertical vector with generator $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$, by [F5]. Reproduction and equivariance in [F4] yield $A_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+\theta_L(g_{ij})$. [A1, F1, F4, F5, F6, F7, F9, step 1.1]
items/def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative.md:126:2.1 Put $g=g_{ij}(x)$, $Z=\theta_L(g)(X)$, and $R=\rho(g^{-1})$. Along a curve through $x$ tangent to $X$, the transition curve has initial tangent $d(L_g)_eZ$, by [F5]. The curve $t\mapsto g\exp_G(tZ)$ has the same initial value and tangent, so the differentials of smooth composites along these curves agree by [F6]. Its inverse is exactly $\exp_G(-tZ)g^{-1}$; [F8] and the homomorphism law [F9] therefore give $dR(X)=-\rho_*(Z)R$. Using steps 1.1 and 1.2, $d\psi_j(X)+\rho_*(A_j(X))\psi_j=-\rho_*(Z)R\psi_i+R\,d\psi_i(X)+\rho_*(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}A_i(X))R\psi_i+\rho_*(Z)R\psi_i=R\bigl(d\psi_i(X)+\rho_*(A_i(X))\psi_i\bigr)$, where the first and last $Z$-terms cancel and the adjoint-intertwining identity from step 1.2 gives the final equality. This is precisely the associated-bundle coordinate transition for an $E$-valued 1-form, so the local expressions define one global smooth $E$-valued 1-form and do not depend on the selected sections. [F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F8, F9, step 1.1, step 1.2]
items/def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative.md:128:3.1 In any local chart, multiplication by a scalar function obeys $d(f\psi_i)=df\otimes\psi_i+f\,d\psi_i$; the endomorphism term obeys $\rho_*(A_i)(f\psi_i)=f\rho_*(A_i)\psi_i$ by pointwise linearity. Adding these identities gives $(\nabla^\omega(f\sigma))_i=df\otimes\psi_i+f(\nabla^\omega\sigma)_i$, which is the asserted Leibniz rule in the associated bundle. If $V=0$, every term is zero and the same formulas hold; the argument is pointwise and also applies on an empty base. [F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
