items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:92:## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:116:## Proof
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:118:1.1 For a fundamental field $Z_P$, $\omega(Z_P)=Z$ is constant. The exterior-derivative formula therefore gives $d\omega(Z_P,Y)=Z_P(\omega(Y))-\omega([Z_P,Y])=(\mathcal L_{Z_P}\omega)(Y)$ by [F9]. Differentiating $R_{\exp(tZ)}^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(-tZ)}\omega$ at $t=0$, [F5] yields $\mathcal L_{Z_P}\omega=-[Z,\omega]$. The shuffle convention gives $\tfrac12[\omega\wedge\omega](Z_P,Y)=[Z,\omega(Y)]$, so $\Omega_\omega(Z_P,Y)=0$. Every vertical vector is fundamental by the orbit-differential isomorphism, hence $\Omega_\omega$ is horizontal. [A1, F1, F3, F5, F7, F9]
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:120:1.2 Pullback commutes with $d$, and the fixed linear map $\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}$ preserves the Lie bracket by [F4]. Consequently, for fixed $g\in G$, $R_g^*\Omega_\omega=d(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega)+\tfrac12[\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega\wedge\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega]=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\Omega_\omega$. This is the claimed equivariance. [F1, F3, F4, F7]
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:122:2.1 For horizontal vector fields $X,Y$, $\omega(X)=\omega(Y)=0$, so $\Omega_\omega(X,Y)=d\omega(X,Y)=-\omega([X,Y])$. Meanwhile $\omega(K_H(X,Y))=-\omega(v([X,Y]))=-\omega([X,Y])$, since $\omega$ vanishes on the horizontal component. Both $\Omega_\omega$ and $K_H$ depend only on the horizontal components of their inputs, so the equality extends to all $X,Y$: $\Omega_\omega=\omega\circ K_H$. This fixes the sign relative to the imported vertical-valued curvature. [F1, F2, step 1.1]
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:124:2.2 On an overlap, differentiate $s_j(x)=s_i(x)g_{ij}(x)$ along $X\in T_xM$. The derivative is the sum of $d(R_{g_{ij}(x)})ds_i(X)$ and the fundamental vertical vector at $s_j(x)$ generated by $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$, since $g_{ij}(x)^{-1}g_{ij}(x(t))$ has initial tangent $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$ at the identity. Applying $\omega$, its right equivariance and reproduction property give $A_j(X)=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i(X)+\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$. [F1, F6, F8]
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:126:3.1 By [F7], pullback along $s_i$ commutes with the componentwise exterior derivative; the bracket is pointwise and pullback preserves its alternating arguments. Thus $F_i=s_i^*\Omega_\omega=dA_i+\tfrac12[A_i\wedge A_i]$. On the overlap, the same differentiated-section decomposition as in step 2.2 has a vertical term, which vanishes when inserted into $\Omega_\omega$ by step 1.1. Its remaining term is transformed by the equivariance in step 1.2, yielding $F_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}F_i$. [F1, F3, F7, F8, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.2]
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:128:4.1 By [F7] and the graded-derivation rule in [F3], $d\Omega_\omega=\tfrac12 d[\omega\wedge\omega]=[d\omega\wedge\omega]$, because graded skew-symmetry gives $[\omega\wedge d\omega]=-[d\omega\wedge\omega]$. Hence $D_\omega\Omega_\omega=d\Omega_\omega+[\omega\wedge\Omega_\omega]=\tfrac12[\omega\wedge[\omega\wedge\omega]]=0$, the last equality being graded Jacobi from [F3] and the Jacobi identity in [F1]. Pulling back by each $s_i$ gives $D_{A_i}F_i=dF_i+[A_i\wedge F_i]=0$. [F1, F3, F7]
items/lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi.md:130:5.1 The statements include the zero-dimensional or abelian Lie-algebra cases: then the corresponding brackets vanish, and each displayed identity reduces to the ordinary exterior-derivative identity. A zero-dimensional base has no nonzero 2-forms, and the formulas remain valid. Only the declared $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is inherited from [F1] and [F2]; no global section or full choice principle is assumed. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F7, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, step 4.1] ∎
